{
  "dom_id": "step-q142070",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/84e5e0eb9f97521c7c9fb57c79b5bda67a1747fd0e608f7b466f20acc78ca308.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0?$",
  "worked_solution": "The derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$, $x=c$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14029011/assets/d5e3e8e54b3c3c7f055f82433c6f8a68ffbc4590230fb827a0b8713fdd808a40.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($\u2323$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($\u2322$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$, $x=c$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($\u2323$) at $x=c$ only.",
